<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1712">1712</a>–<a href="/years/1786">1786</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Frederick II of Prussia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick the Great, Frederick II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, was king from 1740. That year he invaded Silesia, citing old Hohenzollern claims to duchies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krnov">Krnov</a>, and won most of it with the County of Kladsko in 1742. He occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> when its princely line ended in 1744, held Silesia through the Seven Years' War and gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a> in 1772. He was also a reforming ruler and patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great.jpg" alt="Frederick the Great" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Georg Ziesenis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_der_Gro%C3%9Fe_-_Johann_Georg_Ziesenis_-_Google_Cultural_Institute_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Silesian Wars Saxony was defeated by Prussia at Kesselsdorf in 1745, and in 1756 <b>Frederick the Great</b> invaded without warning; the Saxon army capitulated at Pirna and was forced into Prussian service, and the country remained occupied for most of the Seven Years’ War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The monarchy then lost Silesia to <b>Frederick II of Prussia</b> in the War of the Austrian Succession, failed to recover it in the Seven Years’ War despite victory at Kolín in 1757, and suffered repeated defeats by revolutionary France after 1792.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia’s first king was only ‘King in Prussia’, because part of Prussia still belonged to Poland; <b>Frederick II</b> took the title ‘King of Prussia’ after the partition of 1772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1701 the army that the electors had built became the army of the Kingdom of Prussia, with which <b>Frederick the Great</b> conquered Silesia from 1740 and survived the Seven Years’ War of 1756–1763.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick the Great</b>'s siege of Olomouc failed in 1758, Napoleon won the Battle of Austerlitz near Brno in 1805, and in 1866 Prussian troops occupied Brno before the preliminary peace of the Austro-Prussian War was signed at Mikulov (Nikolsburg).</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick the Great</b> of Prussia corresponded with Voltaire and kept him at his court from 1750 to 1753; Catherine the Great of Russia based her Instruction of 1767 on Montesquieu and Beccaria; in Portugal the Marquis of Pombal rebuilt Lisbon after the earthquake on a regular plan and…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1760" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1760">What was here in 1760? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for in this Country every man must get to Heaven in his own way.&#8221;</p><footer>Marginal note on religious toleration, June 1740 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; quoted in Thomas Carlyle, History of Friedrich II of Prussia &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2111/pg2111-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I intend, in spite of the Rules of Art, to attack Prince Karl's Army, which is nearly thrice our strength, wherever I find it.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to his generals at Parchwitz, December 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; as taken down at the meeting, quoted in Thomas Carlyle, History of Friedrich II of Prussia &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Happy the moment when I took to training myself in philosophy! There is nothing else that can sustain the soul in a situation like mine.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his sister Wilhelmine, July 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am in the case of a traveller who sees himself surrounded and ready to be assassinated by a troop of cut-throats, who intend to share his spoils.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his sister Wilhelmine, July 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Happy, my dear Sister, is the obscure man, whose good sense from youth upwards, has renounced all sorts of glory; who, in his safe low place, has none to envy him, and whose fortune does not excite the cupidity of scoundrels!&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his sister Wilhelmine, July 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Gentlemen, I am undertaking a war in which I have no allies but your valor and your good will. My cause is just; my resources are what we ourselves can do; and the issue lies in fortune.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to his generals before invading Silesia, December 1740 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/To_His_Generals_Before_Invading_Silesia" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
